--TEST--
`Core\Topic::subscribe` and `unsubscribe` refuse a program that is not a connection, rather than recording a subscription nothing could ever drain — `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-loop` and `rule:core-classes/topic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4 makes a topic the only way two *connections* meet, and § 3 makes the one
// wait that drains a delivery `Core\Socket::receive()` -- which only a script
// an upgrade opened can call. A subscription made anywhere else would therefore
// be an entry in the bus's table with no reader behind it, and the value
// published to it would be copied, queued and never seen. So the refusal is the
// member's answer rather than a silent no-op: a command-line script that meant
// to be a connection learns so at the call it got wrong.
try {
    Core\Topic::subscribe("room:lobby");
    echo "subscribed\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The pair cannot disagree about what a call means, so leaving a topic is
// refused by the same question in the same wording -- and not, for instance,
// answered as though the connection had simply never joined it.
try {
    Core\Topic::unsubscribe("room:lobby");
    echo "unsubscribed\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
`Core\Topic::subscribe` needs a connection and this program is not one: only a script `Core\Socket::upgrade` opened runs inside a connection isolate
`Core\Topic::unsubscribe` needs a connection and this program is not one: only a script `Core\Socket::upgrade` opened runs inside a connection isolate
